1
00:00:00,240 --> 00:00:02,929
Two people start managing teams at twenty-four

2
00:00:02,929 --> 00:00:03,981
Age proves nothing

3
00:00:03,981 --> 00:00:06,027
What is odd is that they have staff

4
00:00:06,027 --> 00:00:07,488
and the staff work nights

5
00:00:08,068 --> 00:00:10,428
The piece lays out three arrangements

6
00:00:10,428 --> 00:00:11,449
AI underneath us

7
00:00:11,449 --> 00:00:12,788
beside us, between us

8
00:00:12,788 --> 00:00:14,765
All three have been said before

9
00:00:14,765 --> 00:00:17,572
This time the subject is who reports to whom

10
00:00:18,152 --> 00:00:20,210
The first, a basement full of AI

11
00:00:20,210 --> 00:00:21,239
Your title stays

12
00:00:21,239 --> 00:00:22,911
a floor appears underneath

13
00:00:22,911 --> 00:00:24,775
First you supervise an intern

14
00:00:24,775 --> 00:00:26,576
Eventually you run a company

15
00:00:27,156 --> 00:00:29,688
The accounting example is the one I keep

16
00:00:29,688 --> 00:00:32,030
Nine tenths of the work is taken over

17
00:00:32,030 --> 00:00:34,309
and the job left is ten times bigger

18
00:00:34,309 --> 00:00:35,575
I like that sentence

19
00:00:35,575 --> 00:00:36,588
I trust it least

20
00:00:37,168 --> 00:00:38,651
The second, colleagues

21
00:00:38,651 --> 00:00:39,931
The wider the scope

22
00:00:39,931 --> 00:00:41,212
the harder to build

23
00:00:41,212 --> 00:00:42,425
the easier to sell

24
00:00:42,425 --> 00:00:44,244
A tool needs the whole firm

25
00:00:44,244 --> 00:00:45,592
A team needs one nod

26
00:00:46,172 --> 00:00:48,472
The authors push back on themselves

27
00:00:48,472 --> 00:00:51,034
Economies are not built by solo workers

28
00:00:51,034 --> 00:00:52,020
For AI to count

29
00:00:52,020 --> 00:00:54,188
it has to meet the bar of a group

30
00:00:54,768 --> 00:00:56,969
The third is hardest to picture

31
00:00:56,969 --> 00:00:58,105
Human, AI, human

32
00:00:58,105 --> 00:00:59,880
with the AI in the middle

33
00:00:59,880 --> 00:01:01,655
It reads the board up top

34
00:01:01,655 --> 00:01:03,288
and hands the plan down

35
00:01:03,868 --> 00:01:05,176
The example is a CEO

36
00:01:05,176 --> 00:01:08,316
Sales promised a feature engineering never built

37
00:01:08,316 --> 00:01:09,690
Nobody wrote a report

38
00:01:09,690 --> 00:01:10,867
The advisor saw it

39
00:01:10,867 --> 00:01:12,241
and found an engineer

40
00:01:12,241 --> 00:01:13,876
who had done unasked work

41
00:01:14,456 --> 00:01:15,770
Advisors are not new

42
00:01:15,770 --> 00:01:16,559
The price is

43
00:01:16,559 --> 00:01:18,991
Consulting firms stood above the firm

44
00:01:18,991 --> 00:01:20,503
and charged by the hour

45
00:01:20,503 --> 00:01:22,080
This one is cheap enough

46
00:01:22,080 --> 00:01:23,000
to never leave

47
00:01:23,580 --> 00:01:25,833
The piece ends with four questions

48
00:01:25,833 --> 00:01:27,225
More or less red tape

49
00:01:27,225 --> 00:01:28,750
bigger or smaller firms

50
00:01:28,750 --> 00:01:30,473
It keeps both answers open

51
00:01:30,473 --> 00:01:32,196
I would keep them open too

52
00:01:32,776 --> 00:01:34,396
The line I would copy out

53
00:01:34,396 --> 00:01:35,627
The wider the scope

54
00:01:35,627 --> 00:01:36,858
the harder to build

55
00:01:36,858 --> 00:01:38,090
the easier to adopt

56
00:01:38,090 --> 00:01:39,904
It explains more than itself

57
00:01:40,484 --> 00:01:41,644
So I do my own sum

58
00:01:41,644 --> 00:01:44,220
One extra floor means one extra handover

59
00:01:44,220 --> 00:01:45,380
Handovers are free

60
00:01:45,380 --> 00:01:47,313
but someone keeps the light on

61
00:01:47,313 --> 00:01:49,052
and someone keeps the books
